# Batch 11 (2026-10-23): Iran/Persia, Korea and Japan, Ethiopia/Horn of Africa and Madagascar, the Balkans, ancient Egypt gaps,
# Greco-Roman world, Anglo-Saxon/Norse Britain and Scandinavia, deep time and 20th–21st-century anomalies and hoaxes
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[
# --- part 1: Iran/Persia and Korea
X('zarathustra','Zarathustra (Zoroaster): a prophet nobody can date',H,'disputed',2,-1200,['textual (Avestan, Greek)','linguistic'],
 "Zarathustra (Greek Zoroaster) is the prophet of Zoroastrianism, the ancient religion of Iran centred on the wise lord Ahura Mazda and a cosmic struggle between truth (asha) and the lie (druj). The oldest texts attributed to him are the Gathas, hymns in Old Avestan preserved in the Avesta. Almost nothing about his life can be confirmed independently, and his date is one of the widest-ranging in the history of religion. Because the language of the Gathas is very close to that of the Rigveda, many linguists place him somewhere around 1500–1000 BCE; a later Zoroastrian tradition dates him \"258 years before Alexander\", i.e. around the 6th century BCE. Zoroastrianism became the main religion of the Achaemenid, Parthian and Sasanian empires and survives today among Parsis and Iranian Zoroastrians.",
 [('Early date','Many Iranists (for example Mary Boyce) place him in the late 2nd millennium BCE, among pastoral peoples of Central Asia, based on the archaic language and the society described in the Gathas.'),
  ('Late date','Others follow the traditional \"258 years before Alexander\" figure and place him in the 7th–6th century BCE, close to the rise of the Persian Empire.'),
  ('Fabulous antiquity','Some Greek writers, quoted by Pliny the Elder, placed Zoroaster thousands of years before Plato; scholars regard these figures as legendary.'),
  ('Influence on other religions','Scholars debate how far Zoroastrian ideas such as a final judgement, resurrection, a saviour figure and heaven and hell influenced post-exilic Judaism, Christianity and Islam; some see strong influence, others parallel development.')],
 ['wiki:Zoroaster','wiki:Gathas','wiki:Zoroastrianism'],'Zoroaster',dateNote='Proposed dates range from c. 1500 BCE to the 6th century BCE'),
X('jamshid-var','Yima\'s Var: the Persian shelter built against a world-ending winter',M,'myth-legend',2,-500,['textual (Avestan, Middle Persian, Persian epic)','oral tradition'],
 "In the Vendidad (Videvdad), a Zoroastrian text in the Avesta, the god Ahura Mazda warns Yima, the first king, that terrible winters are coming that will destroy most life. He tells Yima to build a var, an enclosure, and to bring into it the seeds of the best men and women, animals, plants and fires, so that they can repopulate the world afterwards. Yima, who presides over a golden age without death or sickness, is the same figure as Jamshid in later Persian tradition, celebrated in Ferdowsi's Shahnameh (c. 1010 CE) as a great king who fell through pride. He is linguistically cognate with Yama, the first mortal and king of the dead in Indian tradition.",
 [('Ark parallel','Many writers note the similarity to Noah, Utnapishtim and Manu: a chosen survivor saves living things from a world-ending disaster; here the disaster is cold rather than water.'),
  ('Ice Age memory?','Bal Gangadhar Tilak\'s The Arctic Home in the Vedas (1903) used the Vendidad\'s winters to argue for an Arctic homeland of the early Indo-Europeans; this idea is not accepted by mainstream scholars and is regarded as speculative.'),
  ('Mythic winter','Scholars usually read the var as a mythic paradise or a cosmological story rather than a memory of a specific climate event; comparisons are drawn with the Norse Fimbulwinter before Ragnarök.')],
 ['wiki:Yima','wiki:Jamshid','wiki:Vendidad'],'Jamshid',dateNote='Text date uncertain (Avestan oral tradition, written down in the Sasanian era); story older'),
X('shahr-e-sukhteh','Shahr-e Sukhteh, the "Burnt City": an artificial eye and a 5,000-year-old animation?',H,'confirmed',2,-3200,['archaeological','UNESCO documentation'],
 "Shahr-e Sukhteh (\"Burnt City\") in Sistan and Baluchestan, south-east Iran, was a large Bronze Age urban settlement occupied from about 3200 BCE until roughly 2000 BCE, and burned several times during its history. Excavations since the 1960s (Italian and later Iranian teams) found evidence of crafts, long-distance trade with Mesopotamia, Central Asia and the Indus, and well-preserved organic remains. Famous finds include an artificial eyeball found in 2006 with the skeleton of a tall woman, dated about 2900–2800 BCE, a game board with dice, and an earthenware goblet painted with a series of images of a goat leaping towards a tree. It was inscribed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2014.",
 [('First animation?','When the goblet\'s five images are viewed in sequence they appear to show the goat leaping; Iranian archaeologists have called it the earliest known animation. Others think the repetition is decorative and that calling it animation is a modern reading.'),
  ('Ancient surgery','Excavators have reported a child\'s skull with signs of surgery, presented as early brain surgery; these claims are discussed mainly in Iranian publications and press reports and are less widely examined in international literature.'),
  ('Lost civilisation?','Together with Jiroft, Shahr-e Sukhteh is cited as evidence of a sophisticated eastern Iranian Bronze Age culture largely unknown before the late 20th century; it is a lost civilisation in the sense of being forgotten, not mysterious.')],
 ['wiki:Shahr-e_Sukhteh','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1456/|UNESCO World Heritage: Shahr-i Sokhta|heritage'],'Shahr-e_Sukhteh',end=-2000,dateNote='c. 3200 – c. 2000 BCE'),
X('behistun','The Behistun inscription: a king\'s cliff boast that unlocked cuneiform, and the impostor he killed',H,'confirmed',2,-520,['inscription','textual'],
 "At Mount Behistun (Bisotun) in western Iran, about 100 metres up a limestone cliff above an ancient road, Darius I of Persia had a relief and a long inscription carved around 520 BCE in three languages and scripts: Old Persian, Elamite and Babylonian (Akkadian). It tells how Darius came to power after defeating Gaumata, a Magian priest who, he says, had pretended to be Bardiya (Smerdis), the brother of the previous king Cambyses. Copied at great risk by Henry Rawlinson between 1835 and 1847, the trilingual text became the key to deciphering cuneiform, much as the Rosetta Stone was for Egyptian hieroglyphs. In 1857 the Royal Asiatic Society asked four scholars (Rawlinson, Edward Hincks, Julius Oppert and William Henry Fox Talbot) to translate a newly found Assyrian text independently; their versions agreed closely, convincing the public that Akkadian could be read.",
 [('Darius\'s account','Darius says the real Bardiya had been secretly killed by Cambyses and that Gaumata was an impostor; Herodotus tells a similar story of a false Smerdis.'),
  ('Usurper\'s propaganda?','Many historians suggest that Darius himself may have killed the genuine Bardiya and invented the impostor story to justify a usurpation; the evidence allows both readings.'),
  ('Decipherment','The 1857 translation test is often described as one of the first deliberate tests of a decipherment; the convergence of the four versions is still cited as evidence that cuneiform was really understood.')],
 ['wiki:Behistun_inscription','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1222/|UNESCO World Heritage: Bisotun|heritage'],'Behistun_Inscription',
 changed='c. 520 BCE: carved. 1835–47: copied by Rawlinson. 1857: independent translation test confirms cuneiform decipherment.'),
X('cyrus-cylinder','The Cyrus Cylinder: the "first charter of human rights"?',H,'confirmed',2,-539,['archaeological','inscription','textual'],
 "The Cyrus Cylinder is a clay cylinder inscribed in Akkadian cuneiform, made after Cyrus the Great of Persia captured Babylon in 539 BCE. It was found in 1879 by Hormuzd Rassam at Babylon and is in the British Museum. In the traditional style of Mesopotamian building inscriptions, it condemns the previous king Nabonidus, presents Cyrus as chosen by the god Marduk, and describes him restoring temples and returning deported peoples and their gods to their homes. In 2010 the British Museum identified fragments of copies of the same text on tablets in its collection. The Hebrew Bible (Ezra) also credits Cyrus with allowing exiled Jews to return to Jerusalem and rebuild the Temple, although the cylinder does not mention Jews.",
 [('Human-rights charter','In 1971 the Shah of Iran promoted the cylinder as \"the first charter of human rights\", and a replica is displayed at the United Nations; this view remains popular in Iran and elsewhere.'),
  ('Royal propaganda','Most historians see it as a skilful piece of Mesopotamian royal propaganda in a long-established genre, legitimising a foreign conqueror; it does not set out universal rights.'),
  ('Biblical connection','Scholars debate whether the cylinder\'s general policy of repatriation supports the Bible\'s account of a decree for the Jews (Ezra 1 and 6) or only shows that such a policy was plausible.')],
 ['wiki:Cyrus_Cylinder'],'Cyrus_Cylinder',
 changed='1879: found at Babylon. 1971: promoted as a human-rights charter. 2010: further copies of the text identified.'),
X('cambyses-army','The lost army of Cambyses: 50,000 men swallowed by the desert?',H,'disputed',2,-524,['textual (Herodotus)','archaeological claims'],
 "According to Herodotus (Histories 3.26), the Persian king Cambyses II, after conquering Egypt, sent 50,000 soldiers from Thebes to attack the Ammonians of the Siwa oasis and burn the oracle of Amun. The army reached an oasis seven days' march into the desert, then vanished: the Ammonians said a great southern wind buried it under sand. No trace of the army has been confirmed, and the story has become one of the desert's best-known mysteries.",
 [('Buried by a sandstorm','The traditional view takes Herodotus at face value. In 2009 Italian explorers Angelo and Alfredo Castiglioni announced finds of bones, weapons and other objects they linked to the army; these claims were widely reported but not confirmed in peer-reviewed archaeology, and Egyptian authorities were sceptical.'),
  ('Defeated, not buried','Egyptologist Olaf Kaper (Leiden) argued in 2014 that the army was in fact defeated by an Egyptian rebel leader, Petubastis III, and that the sandstorm tale was later Persian face-saving; this is a hypothesis based on inscriptions from the Dakhla oasis.'),
  ('Exaggeration','Other historians suggest that the size of the army, or the whole episode, may be exaggerated in Herodotus, who wrote around 70 years later and portrays Cambyses as mad.')],
 ['wiki:Lost_Army_of_Cambyses','wiki:Petubastis_III'],'Lost_Army_of_Cambyses',dateNote='c. 524 BCE'),
X('qanats','Qanats: Persia\'s underground rivers and their world-wide spread',H,'confirmed',2,-800,['archaeological','textual','UNESCO documentation'],
 "A qanat is a gently sloping underground tunnel that taps groundwater at the foot of hills and carries it by gravity, sometimes for tens of kilometres, to fields and towns, with a line of vertical shafts for digging and maintenance. Developed in Iran in the early 1st millennium BCE (or possibly earlier), the technique spread under the Persian empires and later Islamic and other states, from Oman (aflaj) and North Africa (foggaras) to Spain and the Turpan oasis in China (karez). The Qasabeh qanat at Gonabad, said to date from c. 700–500 BCE, has 427 wells and a total length of about 33 km. Eleven Iranian qanats were inscribed by UNESCO as \"The Persian Qanat\" in 2016.",
 [('Where invented?','Most scholars credit Iran, but some archaeologists argue that similar systems in south-east Arabia (the UAE and Oman) date to the early Iron Age and may have developed independently or earlier; the dating evidence is debated.'),
  ('Water and empire','Historians link qanats to Achaemenid policy: Polybius records that the Persians granted tax privileges to those who brought water to dry land, and Persian engineers built qanats in Egypt\'s Kharga oasis.'),
  ('Fossil water','Like the Garamantes\' foggaras in the Sahara, qanats can deplete ancient groundwater; many have dried up in recent decades as deep pumped wells lower the water table.')],
 ['wiki:Qanat','wiki:Qanats_of_Ghasabeh','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1506/|UNESCO World Heritage: The Persian Qanat|heritage'],'Qanat',dateNote='Early 1st millennium BCE onwards; still in use'),
X('alamut-assassins','Alamut and the Assassins: hashish gardens and the Old Man of the Mountain',H,'disputed',2,1090,['textual (Persian, Arabic, Crusader, Marco Polo)','archaeological'],
 "In 1090 the Ismaili missionary Hasan-i Sabbah took the mountain fortress of Alamut in northern Iran and founded the Nizari Ismaili state, a network of castles in Iran and Syria that survived until the Mongols took Alamut in 1256. Facing far larger Seljuk armies, the Nizaris used targeted killings of political and military leaders, which made them notorious. Their enemies called them hashishiyya, a term of abuse that reached Europe through the Crusaders as \"Assassins\". The Mongols destroyed Alamut's famous library, though the historian Juvayni saved some works first.",
 [('Hashish and paradise legend','Marco Polo (c. 1300) and Crusader writers described the \"Old Man of the Mountain\" drugging young men and placing them in a secret garden of paradise so they would die for him; tales also told of followers leaping to their deaths on command.'),
  ('Legends of enemies','Modern historians of the Ismailis, notably Farhad Daftary, argue that these \"Assassin legends\" were invented or embellished by hostile Sunni and Crusader writers and that there is no evidence the Nizaris used hashish.'),
  ('Lasting myth','The legends shaped the word \"assassin\" in European languages and modern fiction; the Nizari Ismaili community continues today under the Aga Khan.')],
 ['wiki:Order_of_Assassins','wiki:Alamut_Castle','wiki:Hasan-i_Sabbah'],'Alamut_Castle',end=1256,dateNote='1090–1256 (Alamut period)'),
X('jikji','Jikji: the oldest surviving book printed with movable metal type, 78 years before Gutenberg',H,'confirmed',2,1377,['textual','bibliographic','museum collection'],
 "Jikji (in full Baegun Hwasang Chorok Buljo Jikji Simche Yojeol) is a Korean anthology of Zen (Seon) Buddhist teachings printed with movable metal type at Heungdeok temple near Cheongju in 1377, during the Goryeo dynasty. It is the oldest existing book printed with movable metal type, about 78 years older than the Gutenberg Bible. Only the second of its two volumes survives; it was acquired in Korea in the late 19th century by the French diplomat Victor Collin de Plancy and is held by the Bibliothèque nationale de France, where the Korean scholar Park Byeong-seon identified its significance in 1972. It was added to UNESCO's Memory of the World Register in 2001.",
 [('East Asian priority','Movable type itself was invented earlier in China by Bi Sheng (c. 1040, ceramic type), and Korean records describe metal-type printing in the 13th century, but no earlier metal-type books survive.'),
  ('Did it reach Europe?','Some writers have suggested that knowledge of East Asian printing reached Gutenberg through trade or travellers; there is no direct evidence, and most historians see European printing as an independent development. This connection remains speculative.'),
  ('Ownership','Korea has sought the return of Jikji; France holds it as legally acquired. The dispute is part of a wider debate about cultural heritage held abroad.')],
 ['wiki:Jikji','wiki:Movable_type'],'Jikji'),
X('egg-born-kings','Korea\'s egg-born kings: Jumong, Bak Hyeokgeose and Kim Suro',M,'myth-legend',2,-57,['textual (Korean chronicles)','inscription','oral tradition'],
 "Korean founding legends repeatedly describe kings hatched from eggs. Jumong (Dongmyeong), founder of Goguryeo (traditionally 37 BCE), was born from an egg laid by Lady Yuhwa, daughter of the river god Habaek, after she was touched by sunlight. Bak Hyeokgeose, founder of Silla (traditionally 57 BCE), hatched from a large egg found beside a well where a white horse knelt. Kim Suro, founder of Geumgwan Gaya (traditionally 42 CE), came from one of six golden eggs lowered from the sky in a box. The stories are recorded in the Samguk sagi (1145) and Samguk yusa (late 13th century); the Gwanggaeto Stele of 414 CE already states that Goguryeo's founder came out of an egg.",
 [('Sacred kingship','Scholars read the eggs as markers of heavenly origin that legitimised royal lineages, linking rulers to the sun or sky; similar motifs appear in Manchurian and other north-east Asian traditions.'),
  ('Wider pattern','Egg births are widespread in world myth: the cosmic egg of Pangu, the Hindu Hiranyagarbha, the Orphic egg, Leda\'s eggs in Greek myth; whether these reflect diffusion or independent invention is debated.'),
  ('Historical kernel','Historians accept that Goguryeo, Silla and Gaya were real states, but the traditional founding dates, especially Silla\'s early date, are considered unreliable.')],
 ['wiki:Dongmyeong_of_Goguryeo','wiki:Hyeokgeose_of_Silla','wiki:Suro_of_Geumgwan_Gaya','wiki:Gwanggaeto_Stele'],'Bak_Hyeokgeose',dateNote='Traditional dates 57 BCE (Silla), 37 BCE (Goguryeo), 42 CE (Gaya)'),
]
E+=[
# --- part 2: Korea, Japan, Ethiopia/Horn of Africa, Madagascar
X('turtle-ships','Turtle ships: Korea\'s armoured warships and the "first ironclad" debate',H,'disputed',2,1592,['textual (Joseon annals, Yi Sun-sin\'s war diary)','later illustrations'],
 "The geobukseon (turtle ship) was a Korean warship with a covered deck, often shown with spikes and a dragon head at the bow. Ships called gwiseon are mentioned in the Annals of the Joseon Dynasty in 1413 and 1415. An improved design was used by Admiral Yi Sun-sin against the Japanese invasions of 1592–1598 ordered by Toyotomi Hideyoshi, beginning at the Battle of Sacheon in 1592, alongside the main panokseon warships. Yi's fleet won a series of victories that cut Japanese supply lines. No original turtle ship survives, and searches for wrecks have so far not found one.",
 [('First ironclad','Popular accounts in Korea and elsewhere describe the turtle ship as the world\'s first ironclad, with a roof of iron plates.'),
  ('Uncertain evidence','Many historians note that Yi Sun-sin\'s own reports and other contemporary Korean sources do not clearly describe iron plating; later illustrations show hexagonal plates that could be iron or wood. The armour question is unresolved.'),
  ('Lost wrecks','Several surveys of the seabed near battle sites have reported possible remains, but none has been confirmed as a turtle ship.')],
 ['wiki:Turtle_ship','wiki:Yi_Sun-sin','wiki:Imjin_War'],None,end=1598,dateNote='Mentioned 1413; famous in the Imjin War 1592–1598'),
X('himiko-yamatai','Himiko, the shaman queen of Yamatai: Japan\'s lost kingdom',H,'disputed',2,189,['textual (Chinese chronicles)','archaeological'],
 "The Chinese Records of Wei (Wei zhi, part of the Records of the Three Kingdoms compiled by Chen Shou in the late 3rd century) describe the land of Wa (Japan) as unified under Himiko, an unmarried queen of Yamatai who \"occupied herself with magic and sorcery\" (kidō), lived secluded with a thousand female attendants and was served by her younger brother. In 238 or 239 CE she sent envoys to the Wei court, which gave her the title \"Queen of Wa, friendly to Wei\", a gold seal and a hundred bronze mirrors. When she died around 248, a large mound was raised and, the text says, over a hundred attendants followed her in death. Japan's own chronicles, the Kojiki (712) and Nihon Shoki (720), do not name her.",
 [('Kinai (Nara) location','Many archaeologists place Yamatai in the Nara basin and link Himiko to the Makimuku site and the Hashihaka kofun, an early keyhole tomb dated to the mid-3rd century; the tomb is managed by the Imperial Household Agency and cannot be excavated freely.'),
  ('Kyushu location','Others place Yamatai in northern Kyushu, pointing to the Wei zhi\'s itinerary and to large moated settlements such as Yoshinogari; taken literally, the itinerary\'s distances lead into the ocean, so both camps must correct it.'),
  ('Identity','The Nihon Shoki quotes the Wei zhi in its section on Empress Jingū, and some scholars link Himiko with figures such as Yamatototohimomosohime; none of these identifications is proven.')],
 ['wiki:Himiko','wiki:Yamatai','wiki:Hashihaka_Kofun'],'Hashihaka_Kofun',end=248,dateNote='Reigned c. 189 – c. 248 CE (per Chinese sources)'),
X('kitsune-tamamo','Kitsune and Tamamo-no-Mae: the nine-tailed fox and the Killing Stone',M,'myth-legend',2,1150,['textual (Japanese tales, Noh)','oral tradition','folk religion'],
 "In Japanese folklore, kitsune are foxes with magical powers that grow more tails as they age, up to nine, and can take human form, often as beautiful women; some are benevolent messengers of the rice deity Inari, others tricksters. The idea draws on Chinese huli jing (fox spirit) lore. The most famous tale is Tamamo-no-Mae, a brilliant courtesan of a 12th-century emperor (Konoe or Toba, depending on the version) who fell mysteriously ill; the diviner Abe no Yasuchika exposed her as a nine-tailed fox, and warriors killed her on the plain of Nasu. Her body became the Sesshō-seki (Killing Stone), a rock in a volcanic area of Tochigi where poisonous gases rise. The poet Bashō visited it in 1689. On 5 March 2022 the stone was reported to have split in two, probably through weathering, and a ceremony was held to appease the spirit.",
 [('Volcanic origin of the legend','Volcanic gases (such as hydrogen sulphide) at Nasu really do kill small animals near the stone; many see the \"killing\" legend as an explanation of this natural hazard.'),
  ('Fox possession','Kitsunetsuki (fox possession) was a widely reported belief in Japan into the 20th century; psychiatrists and anthropologists have studied it as a culture-bound way of describing illness or social conflict.'),
  ('Shared Asian motif','The nine-tailed fox spirit appears in Chinese, Korean (kumiho) and Japanese tradition; the Tamamo story itself links to Chinese tales of fox consorts who ruined kings.')],
 ['wiki:Kitsune','wiki:Tamamo-no-Mae','wiki:Sesshō-seki'],'Tamamo-no-Mae',dateNote='Set in the 12th century; tale written down from the 14th–17th centuries; stone split 2022'),
X('takeuchi-documents','The Takeuchi documents and the "Tomb of Christ" in northern Japan',H,'debunked',2,1935,['textual (disputed documents)','tourist site'],
 "The Takeuchi documents are texts promoted in the 1920s–30s by Takeuchi Kiyomaro (Kyōmaro), founder of the Kōso Kōtai Jingū religious group, who claimed they were ancient family records written in \"divine characters\". They describe a vast prehistory in which Japan was the centre of the world and figures such as Moses, the Buddha and Jesus came to Japan to serve its emperors. In 1935 Takeuchi identified a grave mound in the village of Herai (now Shingō, Aomori) as the tomb of Christ: according to the story, Jesus studied in Japan, returned to Judea, escaped crucifixion while his brother Isukiri died in his place, and returned to Japan to live to 106. The village has held a \"Christ festival\" every June since 1964, and the site is a tourist attraction.",
 [('Modern forgery','Historian Kanō Kōkichi examined five of the documents in the 1930s and concluded they were modern forgeries; mainstream historians and linguists agree, citing anachronistic language and lack of any earlier provenance.'),
  ('Believers','A small number of followers continue to treat the documents as genuine; the shrine says the originals and sacred treasures are kept privately. The originals have not been made available for independent study.'),
  ('A wider pattern','Stories that Jesus survived or lived abroad also appear elsewhere, for example claims of his tomb at Roza Bal in Srinagar, Kashmir, and Nicolas Notovitch\'s 1894 \"Life of Saint Issa\" set in Tibet; these too are rejected by mainstream historians.')],
 ['wiki:Takeuchi_documents','wiki:Kirisuto_no_Haka','wiki:Shingō,_Aomori'],'Kirisuto_no_Haka',
 changed='1935: tomb announced. 1930s: documents examined and judged forgeries. 1964 onwards: annual festival at the site.'),
X('tsunami-stones','Tsunami stones: Japan\'s warnings carved in rock',H,'confirmed',2,1896,['inscriptions','historical records','eyewitness reports'],
 "Along Japan's north-east coast stand hundreds of stone markers, some said to be over 600 years old, that record past tsunamis, list the dead or warn people where not to build. Most were set up after the 1896 Meiji Sanriku earthquake and tsunami, which killed about 22,000 people, and the 1933 Shōwa Sanriku tsunami. The stone at Aneyoshi in Miyako, Iwate, reads in part: \"High dwellings are the peace and harmony of our descendants. Remember the calamity of the great tsunamis. Do not build any homes below this point.\" In the Tōhoku tsunami of 11 March 2011 the water at Aneyoshi stopped short of the stone, and the hamlet's houses, built above it, survived; elsewhere many towns had spread below old markers, trusting sea walls.",
 [('Generational forgetting','Tohoku University disaster researcher Fumihiko Imamura has observed that \"it takes about three generations for people to forget\"; the stones are studied as an attempt to beat that forgetting.'),
  ('Messages to the future','Researchers compare tsunami stones with other long-term warnings, such as European \"hunger stones\" in river beds and proposals for markers at nuclear-waste sites meant to last 10,000 years.'),
  ('Limits','Many stones were ignored, moved or eroded; after 2011 some argued that ruined buildings should be preserved as warnings in the same way.')],
 ['wiki:Tsunami_stone','wiki:1896_Sanriku_earthquake','wiki:2011_Tōhoku_earthquake_and_tsunami'],'Tsunami_stone',dateNote='Many erected after 1896 and 1933; some much older'),
X('namazu','Namazu: the giant catfish that shakes Japan',M,'myth-legend',3,1855,['woodblock prints','folk religion','textual'],
 "In Japanese folk belief, earthquakes are caused by Ōnamazu, a giant catfish living in the mud beneath the islands. The god Kashima (Takemikazuchi) keeps it pinned down with a great stone, the kaname-ishi, which visitors can still see at Kashima Shrine in Ibaraki; when the god's attention lapses, the catfish thrashes. After the Ansei Edo earthquake of 11 November 1855, which devastated Edo (Tokyo), hundreds of popular woodblock prints called namazu-e appeared, showing the catfish being punished by townspeople, apologising, or, in some prints, as an agent of \"world renewal\" (yonaoshi) that redistributed wealth from the rich to the builders and labourers who profited from reconstruction.",
 [('Animal precursors','The belief that catfish behave strangely before earthquakes has prompted scientific study in Japan; animal-behaviour precursors remain unproven, and no reliable prediction method based on them exists.'),
  ('Social commentary','Historians such as Gregory Smits read the namazu-e as political and economic commentary in late-Tokugawa Japan, expressing hopes and fears about social change.'),
  ('World-supporting creatures','Many cultures explain earthquakes by a creature carrying or under the earth, such as the Hindu world-elephants or the Greek god Poseidon \"Earth-shaker\"; namazu is the Japanese form of this pattern.')],
 ['wiki:Namazu','wiki:1855_Edo_earthquake','wiki:Kashima_Shrine'],'Namazu',dateNote='Belief older; namazu-e prints flourished after the 1855 Edo earthquake'),
X('lucy','Lucy (Dinkinesh): the skeleton that walked upright 3.2 million years ago',H,'confirmed',1,-3200000,['fossil','geological dating'],
 "On 24 November 1974, at Hadar in the Afar region of Ethiopia, Donald Johanson and Tom Gray found the first bones of a partial skeleton (AL 288-1) of Australopithecus afarensis, about 40 percent complete and dated to about 3.2 million years ago. That night the team played the Beatles' \"Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds\", and the skeleton was nicknamed Lucy; in Amharic she is Dinkinesh, \"you are marvellous\". Her pelvis and leg bones show she walked upright, while her brain was ape-sized, settling a major debate: upright walking came long before big brains. She is kept at the National Museum of Ethiopia in Addis Ababa.",
 [('Fall from a tree','A 2016 study (Kappelman and colleagues) proposed that compressive fractures in Lucy\'s bones show she died after falling from a tall tree; Johanson and others replied that such breaks commonly occur during fossilisation.'),
  ('Tree climber too?','Researchers debate how much time A. afarensis spent in trees: curved finger bones and strong arms suggest climbing, while the feet and hips were adapted for walking.'),
  ('Ancestor or cousin','Lucy\'s species is often presented as a likely ancestor of Homo, but newer finds (for example Australopithecus deyiremeda and Kenyanthropus) show several hominin species lived at the same time, making the family tree bushier and the direct line uncertain.')],
 ['doi:10.1038/260293a0','doi:10.1038/nature19332','wiki:Lucy_(Australopithecus)'],'Lucy_(Australopithecus)',dateNote='c. 3.2 million years ago; found 1974',
 changed='1974: found. 1978: species Australopithecus afarensis named. 2007–2013: toured the USA. 2016: tree-fall hypothesis published.'),
X('laas-geel','Laas Geel: the painted cattle of Somaliland',H,'confirmed',2,-3500,['archaeological','rock art'],
 "Laas Geel is a group of rock shelters on the outskirts of Hargeisa in Somaliland whose walls are covered with vivid painted figures, mainly of humped and long-horned cattle with decorated necks and what look like ceremonial robes, together with people with raised arms, dogs and wild animals. A French archaeological team led by Xavier Gutherz documented them in 2002–2003, although local people had known of the site for centuries. The paintings are estimated to be about 5,500 to 4,500 years old and belong to an \"Ethiopian-Arabian\" rock-art style found across the Horn of Africa and southern Arabia. They are among the earliest evidence of cattle herding in the Horn.",
 [('Cattle cult','Archaeologists interpret the decorated cattle and worshipping figures as evidence of a pastoral society in which cattle had religious as well as economic importance, a pattern also seen among later herders of East Africa and in ancient Egypt.'),
  ('Arabian links','The shared style with southern Arabian rock art is read as evidence of contacts across the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden in the 4th–3rd millennia BCE; the direction and nature of the contact are debated.'),
  ('Dating uncertainty','The paintings have not been dated directly; the estimates come from style and regional context, so the age could change with new evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Laas_Geel'],'Laas_Geel',dateNote='Estimated c. 3500–2500 BCE; documented 2002'),
X('madagascar-settlement','The settlement of Madagascar: voyagers from Borneo to Africa\'s great island',H,'disputed',2,500,['genetic','linguistic','archaeological'],
 "Madagascar was one of the last large landmasses settled by people. Its language, Malagasy, is Austronesian, and in 1951 the linguist Otto Dahl showed that its closest relative is Ma'anyan, spoken in south-east Borneo, more than 7,000 km away across the Indian Ocean. Genetic studies show that Malagasy people have roughly mixed Southeast Asian (Austronesian) and East African (Bantu) ancestry; one study (Cox and colleagues, 2012) estimated that the Asian maternal lineages trace back to a small founding group of perhaps about 30 women. Most evidence points to substantial settlement during the 1st millennium CE, followed by the extinction of the island's megafauna, including elephant birds and giant lemurs.",
 [('Much earlier visitors?','A 2018 study (Hansford and colleagues) reported cut marks on elephant-bird bones dated to around 10,500 years ago, suggesting a much older human presence; other researchers question whether the marks were made by people. The question is open.'),
  ('How did they come?','Scholars debate whether settlers sailed directly across the ocean, followed the coasts of the Indian Ocean via India and Arabia, or came in multiple waves; Austronesian outrigger technology made long voyages possible.'),
  ('Accidental or planned','Some suggest that the founding group may have been small and partly accidental (for example drifting boats); others see deliberate trade-linked voyages. Malagasy oral traditions about the first inhabitants, the Vazimba, add another layer.')],
 ['doi:10.1098/rspb.2012.0012','doi:10.1126/sciadv.aat6925','wiki:History_of_Madagascar','wiki:Malagasy_language'],'History_of_Madagascar',dateNote='Main settlement 1st millennium CE (earlier dates debated)'),
X('vazimba-kalanoro','The Vazimba and the kalanoro: Madagascar\'s first people and the little forest folk',M,'myth-legend',3,500,['oral tradition','ethnography'],
 "In popular Malagasy tradition, the Vazimba were the island's first inhabitants, often described as small people who lived in the forests before later arrivals, knew no iron or rice farming, and kept cattle without eating them; their graves and the spirits of Vazimba are still honoured or feared at sacred sites, especially in the central highlands. Malagasy folklore also tells of the kalanoro, small hairy forest beings with long nails and backward-pointing feet, sometimes said to steal children, sometimes to serve people as spirit helpers and healers who speak through mediums.",
 [('Memory of first settlers','Some historians think Vazimba stories preserve memories of earlier waves of Austronesian settlers, absorbed by later arrivals; the idea that they were a distinct short-statured people has not been proven.'),
  ('Ancestors and land','Anthropologists emphasise the Vazimba\'s role as original \"owners of the land\" whose spirits must be respected, a role found in many cultures\' stories of first peoples.'),
  ('Cryptid or spirit','Cryptozoology programmes have searched for the kalanoro as an unknown primate; mainstream researchers treat it as a folklore being, possibly influenced by memories of extinct giant lemurs. A parallel often drawn is Hawaiʻi\'s menehune.')],
 ['wiki:Vazimba','wiki:Kalanoro'],None,dateNote='Undated tradition; refers to the first settlers'),
]
